      {
        "advisory": {
          "id": "RUSTSEC-2026-0339",
          "package": "quinn-proto",
          "title": "Unbounded growth of pending connection ID retirements in quinn-proto",
          "description": "When processing a NEW_CONNECTION_ID frame, quinn-proto queues a RETIRE_CONNECTION_ID frame for\neach connection ID that the peer asked it to retire. The normal path bounds this queue and closes\nthe connection with a CONNECTION_ID_LIMIT_ERROR if the bound would be exceeded. However, the path\nhandling a connection ID that had already been retired pushed onto the queue without applying\nthe limit and without deduplicating.\n\nA peer that has completed the handshake can first send a NEW_CONNECTION_ID frame with a large\n`retire_prior_to` value to advance the receiver's connection ID window, and then repeatedly send\nNEW_CONNECTION_ID frames with sequence numbers below that window. Each such frame adds another\nentry to the queue. The queue is only drained by sending RETIRE_CONNECTION_ID frames, which are\nsubject to congestion control, so a peer that withholds acknowledgements can stall the drain while\ncontinuing to flood, growing the victim's memory use without bound.\n\nThe flaw was corrected in 0.11.17 by applying the same limit on the already-retired path.\n\nThanks to [@scadastrangelove](https://github.com/scadastrangelove) for reporting this issue.",
          "date": "2026-08-17",
